  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nEarly morning observations across northeast and east-central Kansas\nconfirm a cold, dry surface air mass settling across the region as a\nsurface ridge axis shifts eastward toward the Mississippi Valley.\n\nFor today, surface high pressure continues sliding east while weak \nlee troughing develops over the High Plains, causing light easterly \nsurface winds to veer to the southeast at 5 to 10 mph (locally 10 to \n15 mph toward Republic and Cloud Counties). Filtered sunshine through\npassing mid and high-level cloud cover and limited boundary-layer \nmixing will hold afternoon temperatures below mid-November normals, \nwith highs ranging from the upper 40s along the Nebraska border \n(Marysville to Hiawatha) to near 50 degrees along the Kansas River \nValley (Topeka and Lawrence) and lower 50s toward Emporia.\n\nTonight through Sunday evening, attention turns to a southern- \nstream upper-level trough translating eastward across the Southern \nPlains. Deeper Gulf moisture return and strongest synoptic ascent \nremain focused well south of the Kansas-Oklahoma border, keeping \nwidespread heavy rainfall confined to Texas and the Gulf Coast. \nAcross northeast Kansas, dry low-level easterly to southeasterly \nflow will largely limit moisture availability, resulting in mostly \ncloudy skies tonight and slightly milder lows in the upper 20s \nnortheast to the lower and mid 30s elsewhere. Highs Sunday remain \ncool in the upper 40s north to lower 50s south.\n\nEarly to mid-next week, upper-level ridging builds across the \nCentral Plains behind the departing southern-stream wave while \nsurface high pressure anchors over the southeastern CONUS. Persistent\nsoutherly low-level return flow promotes steady warm air advection \nacross Kansas under mostly clear to partly cloudy skies. Afternoon \nhighs moderate into the lower to mid 50s Monday, upper 50s Tuesday, \nand upper 50s to lower 60s by Wednesday, while overnight lows climb \nfrom near freezing Sunday night into the lower 40s by Tuesday night.\n\nBy late next week, the next upper-level trough approaches from the \nwestern CONUS, bringing the next area-wide chance for rain showers \nacross the area. Current official forecasts carry broad, low-end \nshower chances from Wednesday night through Thursday night alongside \nmild temperatures\u2014highs near 60 to the lower 60s Thursday and \novernight lows in the 40s to near 50\u2014before drier and cooler post- \nfrontal air begins filtering into the region by Friday. Uncertainty \nremains elevated through this period regarding trough timing, \namplitude, and the degree of low-level moisture return ahead of the \nsystem.",
